#!/bin/bash
# A second investigator for a bug that won't give up its cause: Gemini 4
# Neon (its reasoning at xhigh) in the Antigravity CLI, given a written
# brief, working beside you, answering in a file.
#
#   tools/dev/agy-help BRIEF.md [DIR]
#
# BRIEF.md: what's wrong and everything known (the olo-debug-help skill,
# .claude/skills/olo-debug-help/SKILL.md, says what a good one holds).
# DIR (the brief's folder by default; somewhere of yours under /var/tmp):
# the helper's scratch, where `answer.md` (its findings) and `notes.md`
# (what it found on the way) are written, its builds in DIR/target.
#
# The helper is an agy in a tmux session, as every agent here is
# (`tools/dev/agents investigate`: this is that), named help-FOLDER: it
# can be read as it works (`tools/dev/agents peek help-FOLDER`), told
# more (`agents say`), and asks leave for what isn't routine (`agents
# approve`). Run this in the background: it prints a line a minute of
# what the helper is doing, and returns when the answer is written (0),
# or as soon as the helper needs you (3: answer it, then run this again
# with the same brief to go on waiting). It can take an hour. A minder
# starts it with `--no-wait` instead (`tools/dev/agy-help BRIEF.md
# --no-wait`): started, it returns, and the minder's `agents watch` is
# the one wait, its questions and its DONE said once. When
# you've read the answer, `tools/dev/agents stop help-FOLDER` (and
# `forget`).
#
# The helper looks and tries things; it doesn't change the working
# tree, commit, push or touch the person's session (its rules go in
# front of every brief). What it says is a lead to check, not a fact.
exec /usr/bin/python3 "$(dirname "$0")/agents" investigate "$@"
